# Overview
# --------
# This code implements a measurement of demonstrative reference across a
# single author's body of fiction. Every token is parsed, and demonstratives
# are classified by grammatical function alone: proximal (this, these) against
# distal (that, those), and pronominal (bare, its referent a stretch of
# discourse) against adnominal (attached to a head noun). Quoted speech is
# located and set aside so that the narrating voice can be counted on its own.
# The resulting per-work rates are then tested for trend, for a changepoint,
# and against the named confounds of dialogue, narrative person, and register.
#
# The instrument specifies nothing stylistic in advance. It classifies by
# part of speech and lemma, and reports what the distribution does across the
# corpus. A supporting analysis divides adnominal demonstratives by the
# concreteness of their head noun, using published norms where available; that
# division is reported as texture, and no headline claim rests upon it.
#
# How the instrument works, in plain terms:
#  1. It parses each work with a dependency model (udpipe), keeping token,
#     lemma, part of speech, morphological features, and head.
#  2. It locates quoted speech from the text's own quotation convention and
#     flags those spans, so narration can be counted separately.
#  3. It classifies every demonstrative on two axes: proximal/distal (lemma)
#     and pronominal/adnominal (part of speech from the parse).
#  4. It aggregates per work and per chunk, and tests: Spearman correlation
#     with year of first publication, a permutation changepoint, a
#     quasi-binomial GLM with narrative person as a covariate, within-work
#     tests where person varies, and a register comparison.
#  5. It extracts ranked concordance lines for each category, so that every
#     figure can be taken back to the sentences that produced it.
#  6. It validates the shipped category definitions against a hand-built
#     ground-truth table, re-derives the published rates from the cache, and
#     checks extracted exhibits verbatim against the source files.
#
# Scripts
# -------
# The repository comprises four scripts, run in sequence.
#
# Script A — Deixis_Pipeline_Code.R
#   Annotation, classification, per-work and per-chunk aggregation, and the
#   full statistical report.
#     1. Setup and Configuration
#     2. Load Required Libraries
#     3. Helper Functions
#     4. Metadata and Concreteness Norms
#     5. Annotation and Classification
#     6. Aggregation
#     7. Analyses
#     8. Report
#
# Script B — Deixis_Evidence_Code.R
#   Descriptive per-1,000-word rates and ranked concordance (KWIC) lines for
#   each category, drawn from narration only.
#     1. Setup and Configuration
#     2. Load Required Libraries
#     3. Category Definitions and Ranking Functions
#     4. Annotation and Concreteness Norms
#     5. Per-Work Extraction
#     6. Descriptive Digest
#
# Script C — Deixis_Validation_Code.R
#   Four independent checks on the shipped definitions, with a single
#   pass/fail report and a stratified sample for manual audit.
#     1. Setup and Configuration
#     2. Embedded Category Definitions
#     3. Part 1 — Ground Truth
#     4. Part 2 — Consistency
#     5. Part 3 — Verbatim
#     6. Part 4 — Audit Sample
#
# Script D — Deixis_Figure1_Code.R
#   The publication figure: proximal share against year of first publication,
#   with the tested changepoint band and the two regime means.
#
# Key Functions
# -------------
#   detect_quote_marker()     - Reads a text's own quotation convention from
#                               its punctuation counts
#   build_dialogue_flags()    - Marks quoted spans, repairing unclosed
#                               quotations at paragraph boundaries
#   classify_demonstratives() - Assigns polarity (proximal/distal) and class
